===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}}

# {{lb|en|biology}}
## The [[total#Adjective|total]] [[mass#Noun|mass]] of a [[living thing]] or a [[part#Noun|part]] thereof (such as a [[cell#Noun|cell]]).
##* {{quote-book|en|author=David A. Guertin; w:David M. Sabatini|entry=Cell Size Control|title=Encyclopedia of Life Sciences|location=Chichester, West Sussex|publisher=[[w:Wiley (publisher)|John Wiley & Sons]]|year=2005|volume=IV|doi=10.1038/npg.els.0003359|isbn=978-0-470-01617-6|passage=Since protein constitutes the majority of the '''biomass''' of a cell, building new protein is a major way that cells increase their size.}}
## The total mass of all, or a [[specified#Adjective|specified]] [[category]] of, living things within a [[specific#Adjective|specific]] [[area]], [[habitat]], etc.
##* {{quote-journal|en|author=Theo Tait|title=Don’t Wear Yum-yum Yellow [review of ''Demon Fish: Travels through the Hidden World of Sharks'' (2012) by Juliet Eilperin]|magazine=w:London Review of Books|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20221204161741/https://www.lrb.co.uk/the-paper/v34/n15/theo-tait/don-t-wear-yum-yum-yellow|location=London|publisher=LRB Ltd.|date=2 August 2012|volume=34|issue=15|issn=0260-9592|oclc=848524821|passage=In the untouched Shangri-la-like Kingman Reef in the Line Islands in the central Pacific, sharks make up 75 per cent of the fish '''biomass'''.}}
# {{senseid|en|fuel}} [[organic matter|Organic matter]] from living things which were [[recently]] [[alive]] (especially [[vegetation]]) [[use#Verb|used]] as a [[fuel#Noun|fuel]] or [[source#Noun|source]] of [[energy]], especially if [[cultivate]]d for that [[purpose#Noun|purpose]]; also, fuel [[produce#Verb|produced]] from such organic matter; [[biofuel]].
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Robert Williams|chapter=Roles for Biomass Energy in Sustainable Development|editors=[[w:Robert H. Socolow|R[obert Harry] Socolow]]; C. Andrews; F. Berkhout; V. Thomas|title=Industrial Ecology and Global Change|location=Cambridge, Cambridgeshire; New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|year=1994|year_published=1997|section=abstract|page=199|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Wb4Du3C_elkC&pg=PA199|isbn=978-0-521-47197-8|passage=Advanced technologies such as gasifier/gas turbine systems for electric power generation and fuel cells for transportation make it possible for '''biomass''' to provide a substantial share of world energy in the decades ahead, at competitive costs.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Stephen|title=COVID-19: Meet the Railway Heroes|magazine=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|date=22 April 2020|issue=|page=41|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860|passage=Among the key contracts she works on is the supply of coal and '''biomass''' to Drax power station and extensive rail operations for British Steel.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Andrew Mourant|title=Reinforced against Future Flooding|magazine=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|date=26 August 2020|issue=|pages=60–61|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860|passage=All vegetation and tree stumps were sent to a local recycling facility to be turned into '''biomass'''.}}

